<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Wesley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1703">1703</a>–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Wesley was an English cleric, theologian and evangelist who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>, a revival movement within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. Born in Epworth and educated at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he led the Holy Club there and served an unsuccessful ministry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. At a Moravian meeting in Aldersgate Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 24 May 1738 he felt his heart strangely warmed, and he then began preaching outdoors and travelling across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland, organising small groups led by unordained itinerant preachers, women and men. Methodists under him opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Barred from many churches and persecuted at first, he was later described as the best-loved man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-wesley.jpg" alt="John Wesley" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After George Romney · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Wesley_by_George_Romney.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Wesley was born on 28 June 1703 (17 June in the old calendar) at Epworth, the fifteenth child of Samuel Wesley, the rector there from 1696, and Susanna Wesley, who bore 19 children, nine of whom lived beyond infancy. He entered Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in June 1720, was ordained a deacon on 19 September 1725 and was elected a fellow of Lincoln College on 17 March 1726. He was ordained a priest on 22 September 1728. On his return to Oxford he became leader of the Holy Club, formed by his brother Charles for study and devout living. It met daily, took Communion every Sunday and from 1730 visited prisoners in gaol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 14 October 1735 the brothers sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a>, who wanted John as minister of the new parish. The mission left Wesley depressed. The Moravian minister Peter Boehler encouraged him to preach faith until he had it, and on 24 May 1738 he had the evangelical conversion later called his Aldersgate experience. He joined the Moravian society in Fetter Lane and visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in August 1738.</p><p>From 1739 clergy, magistrates and sometimes mobs persecuted the Methodists, partly because Wesley flouted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> rules on parish boundaries and on who could preach. He provided chapels, first the New Room in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> and then The Foundery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He refused to leave the Church of England, which he thought nearer the Scriptural plan than any other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He travelled mostly on horseback, preaching two or three times a day. Stephen Tomkins writes that he rode 250,000 miles and preached more than 40,000 sermons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Wesley argued against unconditional election and, in his system known as Wesleyan Arminianism, held that a believer could reach entire sanctification between justification and death. Albert Outler described his method as a quadrilateral of Scripture, tradition, experience and reason, with Scripture first. He wrote, edited or abridged some 400 publications. In 1774 he published Thoughts Upon Slavery, calling slavery the sum of all villainies.</p><p>His health declined sharply towards the end of his life. He gave his last sermon on 23 February 1791 at Leatherhead and died on 2 March 1791. The societies he founded became the dominant form of the independent Methodist movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Wesley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enlightenment writers such as Montesquieu and Adam Smith attacked slavery as unjust or wasteful, and the evangelical leader <b>John Wesley</b> condemned it in his Thoughts upon Slavery of 1774.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Wesley</b>'s Methodists named three foundations of faith: people are &quot;dead in sin&quot; by nature, they are &quot;justified by faith alone&quot;, and faith produces inward and outward holiness.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>During the 18th-century gin craze the middle classes grew critical of drunkenness among the lower classes, and in 1743 <b>John Wesley</b> proclaimed that buying, selling and drinking liquor, unless absolutely necessary, were evils to be avoided.</blockquote><a href="/movement/temperance-movement">Temperance movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words were first printed in 1901 in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, published by <b>John Wesley</b> Work Jr. and his brother Frederick J. Work.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li><li><blockquote>…Myer Lyon, who also sang on the London opera stage as Michael Leoni, sing it at the Great Synagogue of London, and around 1770 wrote &quot;The God of Abraham Praise&quot;, a paraphrase set to the tune now called &quot;Leoni&quot;; <b>John Wesley</b>'s hymnbook printed it, much Christianised, in 1785.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li><li><blockquote>At a meeting in Aldersgate Street, London, <b>John Wesley</b> experienced an assurance of faith while hearing Luther's preface to Romans read.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Mankind in general have gained by the fall of Adam a capacity of attaining more holiness and happiness on earth than it would have been possible for them to attain if Adam had not fallen.&#8221;</p><footer>Sermon, &quot;God's Love to Fallen Man&quot; (1782) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/God%27s_Love_to_Fallen_Man" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
